{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,12]],"date-time":"2026-03-12T05:36:02Z","timestamp":1773293762485,"version":"3.50.1"},"reference-count":23,"publisher":"Wiley","issue":"4","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2018,1,14]],"date-time":"2018-01-14T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1515888000000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/onlinelibrary.wiley.com\/termsAndConditions#vor"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["Brit J Clinical Pharma"],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2018,4]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (CAI), used for treatment of glaucoma, are generally regarded as safe and unconnected with systemic side effects. We report an unusual case of fatigue, metabolic acidosis, and normocytic anaemia associated with ocular administration of the CAI, dorzolamide, in a patient with impaired renal function. In chronic kidney disease, where CAI elimination may be decreased, and patients prone to develop metabolic acidosis, systemic absorption of ocular administered CAI could lead to rare, but potentially serious adverse reaction, that are a consequence of inhibition of extraocular carbonic anhydrase isoenzymes.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1111\/bcp.13499","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2018,1,15]],"date-time":"2018-01-15T04:54:39Z","timestamp":1515992079000},"page":"796-799","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":21,"title":["Metabolic acidosis and anaemia associated with dorzolamide in a patient with impaired renal function"],"prefix":"10.1111","volume":"84","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-1546-6853","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Iva","family":"Hoffmanov\u00e1","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Second Department of Internal Medicine Third Faculty of Medicine, Charles University  Prague Czech Republic"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},